. *------------
. * GENERATE OUTCOME VARIABLE
. * for the outcome variable, we compute the number of hours in household work 
> women
. * pursure on an average day (including week days and the weekend)
. 
. gen hhwork_sat = pli0012_h
(67,373 missing values generated)

. gen hhwork_sun = pli0016_h
(67,373 missing values generated)

. gen hhwork_week = pli0043_h
(67,373 missing values generated)

. 
. replace hhwork_sat = . if hhwork_sat < 0 | hhwork_sat == 24
(277,950 real changes made, 277,950 to missing)

. replace hhwork_sun = . if hhwork_sun < 0 | hhwork_sun == 24
(279,690 real changes made, 279,690 to missing)

. replace hhwork_week = . if hhwork_week < 0 | hhwork_week == 24 // replaces on
> e outlier that reports to do house work 24/7
(18,820 real changes made, 18,820 to missing)

. 
. gen hhwork = 5/7*hhwork_week + 1/5*hhwork_sat + 1/5*hhwork_sun
(348,129 missing values generated)

. 
. 
. * ============================
. * Analysis
. * ============================
. 
. 
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H3 * (Please adapt according to your needs)
. * for panel regressions:
. * here, we regress the number of hours worked in the household by female resp
> ondents on the 
. * number of daughters and controls in a fixed-effects panel data regression. 
> A negative value suggests that 
. * having daughters is associated with women spending less time on housework o
> n an average day.
. 
. xtset pid syear

Panel variable: pid (unbalanced)
 Time variable: syear, 2000 to 2020, but with gaps
         Delta: 1 unit

. xtreg hhwork num_daughters i.num_children age age2 i.syear if num_children > 
> 0 & male == 0, fe vce(cluster pid)
note: 2019.syear omitted because of collinearity.

